reviewer_comments.reviewer_3:
This manuscript, entitled “Axial Oxygen Coordination Drives Spin-Regulated Electron Transfer in Single-Atom Fe Catalysts for Selective Pollutant Transformation,” presents a solid and interesting study. However, several issues regarding experimental design, writing clarity, and result interpretation should be addressed. Detailed comments are provided below:
1. In Lines 114–115, the provided SEM images do not clearly show the spherical cluster morphology described. The authors should include more dispersed and distinct SEM images to better illustrate the morphology of FePc-O-AND and FePc-AND.
2. In Line 117, size distribution diagrams of the nanoparticles should be provided.
3. The nomenclature used for FePc-O-AND materials in the text (e.g., Line 145) is inconsistent with that in the figures. The authors should ensure uniformity throughout.
4. In Lines 158–160, the claim that “X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES, Fig. 1f) ……confirming preserved square-planar Fe-N4 coordination” is not sufficiently supported by the XANES results. Additional evidence or clarification is needed.
5. Detailed electrochemical experimental procedures should be included in the Supporting Information. Conditions for degradation experiments should be clearly stated in the figure captions.
6. In Lines 285–293, more comprehensive evidence is required. For example, through matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization time-of-flight mass spectrometry (MALDI-TOF-MS). If 4-CP is removed via coupling into larger molecules, the authors should attempt to detect polymeric products using solvents such as ethanol or toluene to dissolve oxidation products adsorbed on the materials. Additionally, a preliminary toxicity assessment of the oxidation products is recommended.
7. In Lines 288–293: Since KSCN is known to rapidly consume PMS in heterogeneous/PMS systems, potentially leading to misinterpretation of quenching effects, the authors should clarify whether KSCN directly reacts with PAA, resulting in significant PAA depletion and thus the observed inhibition of 4-CP removal.
8. In Lines 304–307: According to earlier descriptions, FePc-O-AND/PAA achieves complete 4-CP removal within 2 minutes. Why does the control group (0 mM NaClO4) require 5 minutes? Furthermore, the statement that “FePc-O-AND/PAA system exhibited negligible changes in degradation kinetics” is not accurate. First, Fig. 2i does not provide kinetic data; second, if kinetic constants were calculated, a significant inhibitory effect would likely be evident.
9. In Line 401, data on the effect of HA on 4-CP degradation are missing.
10. In Lines 402–403, experiments evaluating the impact of real water matrices (e.g., river water or treated wastewater) on degradation performance should be added. Water quality parameters of these actual water samples should also be provided.
11. In Lines 457–458, data on PAA consumption are missing.
12. Several errors and inconsistencies throughout the manuscript require careful revision. Examples include:
(1) Line 86: “AND were oxidized”;
(2) Line 163: “these findings provide evidence” (not “evidences”);
(3) Line 165: The full term for “NEXAFS” should be provided upon first use;
(4) Lines 209–211: The text description contradicts Supplementary Fig. 17a;
(5) Lines 311–312: The claim that “the degradation performance remained unchanged in both the FePc-O-AND and FePc-AND systems” is not fully consistent with the slight decrease shown in the results. The description should accurately reflect the experimental outcomes;
(6) Line 391: Remove the erroneous word “acts” after “performance.”;
(7) Verb tenses should be checked and made consistent throughout the manuscript.
13. Several issues were identified in the references:
(1) Lines 63–65: Authoritative references should be added where appropriate. This issue occurs in multiple instances;
(2) Line 178: The citation of Reference 30 does not align with the discussion context “which is assigned to hybridized Fe 3d–O 2p orbitals30, 31”. A more suitable reference should be selected;
(3) Lines 594–595 and 636–637: Page numbers are missing for References 24 and 40.
